## 3. Migration scope

Define the migration boundary before implementation.

### Data migration scope

| Data category | Examples | Migration consideration |
|---|---|---|
| Merchant configuration | Merchant ID, payment setup | Confirm target configuration |
| Customer records | Name, email, customer reference | Data minimization and validation |
| Subscriber records | Subscription ID, status | Lifecycle mapping |
| Collection | Product or service grouping | Recreate or map configuration |
| Plan and pricing | Amount, interval, pricing model | Validate target support |
| Billing schedule | Next billing date, interval | Reconcile schedule |
| Billing history | Past obligations | Historical reporting and retention |
| Payment history | Transactions and results | Financial reconciliation |
| Payment credentials | Tokens, mandates, authorization references | Explicit portability validation |
| Failure history | Failed attempts and recovery | Recovery-state mapping |
| Discounts | Coupons and promotional rules | Validate target behavior |
| Customer preferences | Notification preferences | Consent and communication controls |

### Scope categories

Classify every migration object:

- **Migrate:** Move into the target system.
- **Recreate:** Configure again in FlexiBill.
- **Reference only:** Retain in the merchant's historical system.
- **Integrate:** Keep in the merchant system and synchronize.
- **Exclude:** Do not migrate due to irrelevance, legal constraints, or unsupported data.

Do not migrate unnecessary personal or payment data.

