## 5. Migration readiness assessment

Before starting, assess five dimensions.

### A. Business readiness

- What billing model is currently used?
- How many active subscribers exist?
- How many subscriptions are suspended or overdue?
- What is the billing frequency?
- What is the monthly transaction volume?
- What is the merchant's migration deadline?
- Are there contractual commitments to the existing provider?

### B. Data readiness

- Are subscriber IDs unique?
- Are customer records complete?
- Are billing amounts accurate?
- Are next billing dates available?
- Can the merchant export historical records?
- Are failed and overdue obligations identifiable?
- Are duplicates and invalid records understood?

### C. Payment readiness

- Which payment methods are used?
- Are payment tokens portable?
- Is customer reauthorization required?
- Are mandates or recurring permissions transferable?
- Are there payment-method restrictions?
- Has the target payment flow been tested?

### D. Technical readiness

- Are target APIs available?
- Is Member Center API readiness confirmed?
- Is the required customer-facing frontend ready?
- Can the merchant create the required billing obligations?
- Are webhook events and status mappings understood?
- Can duplicate events be handled safely?

### E. Operational readiness

- Is customer communication approved?
- Are exception owners assigned?
- Is rollback or recovery defined?
- Is reconciliation ready?
- Are support teams trained?
- Is a migration monitoring dashboard available?

## 6. Source-to-target data mapping

### Recommended mapping matrix

| Source object | FlexiBill / target object | Mapping action |
|---|---|---|
| Merchant product | Collection | Recreate or map |
| Subscription package | Plan | Recreate or map |
| Subscription price | Plan pricing | Validate pricing model |
| Subscriber | Member / customer reference | Map or create |
| Subscription | Subscription relationship | Recreate or integrate |
| Billing cycle | Recurring configuration | Validate interval |
| Next billing date | Billing schedule | Reconcile and configure |
| Outstanding invoice | Billing obligation | Validate migration method |
| Payment token | Payment credential reference | Separate portability assessment |
| Transaction history | Historical payment reference | Retain or import where supported |
| Coupon | Coupon configuration | Recreate and validate |
| Subscription status | Lifecycle status | Map explicitly |

### Required migration fields

| Field | Description | Required control |
|---|---|---|
| Source Customer ID | Original customer identifier | Preserve mapping |
| Source Subscription ID | Original subscription identifier | Preserve mapping |
| Target Member ID | Target member reference, if applicable | Validate uniqueness |
| Target Subscription ID | New subscription reference | Record mapping |
| Collection ID | Target Collection | Validate |
| Plan ID | Target Plan | Validate |
| Billing Amount | Expected recurring amount | Compare |
| Billing Interval | Frequency | Compare |
| Next Billing Date | Next scheduled obligation | Reconcile |
| Subscription Status | Active, paused, cancelled, etc. | Map to supported state |
| Payment Method Reference | Eligible payment reference | Validate portability |
| Migration Batch ID | Migration grouping | Track |
| Migration Status | Processing result | Monitor |

Do not assume all fields can be stored natively in FlexiBill. Retain source-to-target mappings in the migration staging layer or merchant system when necessary.

## 7. Subscription lifecycle mapping

Source platforms often use different status definitions.

Create an explicit mapping before importing subscribers.

| Source status | Target treatment | Required validation |
|---|---|---|
| Active | Recreate as active where supported | Payment readiness |
| Trial | Recreate or manage externally | Trial behavior |
| Past due | Reconcile outstanding obligation | Recovery policy |
| Suspended | Recreate supported state or manage externally | Lifecycle capability |
| Paused | Validate supported pause behavior | Do not assume native support |
| Cancelled | Historical reference or cancelled state | Prevent unintended rebilling |
| Expired | Historical reference or expired state | Confirm lifecycle behavior |
| Pending activation | Complete onboarding first | Payment authorization |
| Incomplete | Resolve before migration | Customer action |

### Important controls

- Do not reactivate cancelled subscribers unintentionally.
- Do not collect payment for an already settled obligation.
- Do not restart a billing cycle without checking the original schedule.
- Do not assume that a source pause or suspension maps directly to a FlexiBill lifecycle action.
- Do not create duplicate subscriptions for the same commercial relationship.

## 8. Payment credential and authorization migration

This is one of the most critical migration workstreams.

### Payment credential categories

| Category | Migration treatment |
|---|---|
| Raw card number | Never include in ordinary migration files |
| Tokenized card reference | Validate provider and token portability |
| Network token | Validate target support and authorization |
| Bank mandate | Validate transferability and legal requirements |
| Recurring authorization | Validate merchant, channel, and consent scope |
| Customer payment method ID | Do not assume it works in the target platform |
| Expired payment credential | Require an approved update or reauthorization journey |

### Required decision

```text
EXISTING PAYMENT CREDENTIAL
           |
           v
   PORTABILITY CONFIRMED?
        /       \
       /         \
     YES          NO
      |            |
      v            v
PROVIDER-APPROVED  CUSTOMER
TRANSFER PROCESS   REAUTHORIZATION
      |            |
      v            v
TARGET VALIDATION  NEW PAYMENT SETUP
       \           /
        \         /
         v       v
       PAYMENT READINESS
              |
              v
       CONTROLLED ACTIVATION
```

The transfer process must be approved by the relevant payment provider and meet security, consent, and regulatory requirements.

If portability is unavailable, design a customer reauthorization journey.

### Customer reauthorization journey

1. Merchant identifies affected subscribers.
2. Merchant communicates the migration and required customer action.
3. Customer accesses an approved payment setup journey.
4. Customer provides or confirms the required payment authorization.
5. Payment method is validated.
6. Merchant confirms migration readiness.
7. Future billing is activated only after required checks pass.

Do not describe a subscriber as fully migrated while payment authorization remains incomplete.

## 9. Migration strategy options

### Strategy A — Big-bang migration

All eligible subscribers move within one agreed cutover window.

Suitable when:

- Merchant has a manageable subscriber population.
- Data quality is high.
- Payment readiness is confirmed.
- Cutover dependencies are controlled.

Risk: A single migration issue can affect a large customer population.

### Strategy B — Phased migration

Move subscribers in controlled batches.

Possible batches:

- By product.
- By customer segment.
- By payment method.
- By billing date.
- By country.
- By merchant business unit.

Suitable for larger merchants and complex operating environments.

### Strategy C — Cohort migration

Move subscribers according to their next billing date.

Example:

| Cohort | Billing window | Action |
|---|---|---|
| Cohort A | Next 7 days | Early controlled migration |
| Cohort B | Days 8–14 | Second migration wave |
| Cohort C | Days 15–30 | Third migration wave |
| Cohort D | More than 30 days | Later migration wave |

The cohort approach reduces the risk of disrupting a large number of simultaneous billing events.

Actual cutover windows must account for payment authorization, operational capacity, and source-system behavior.

## 10. Migration execution workflow

### Stage 1 — Discovery

- Identify merchant and source system.
- Confirm commercial model.
- Establish subscriber population.
- Review contracts and provider constraints.
- Confirm target FlexiBill model.
- Identify country-specific requirements.

**Exit gate:** Migration scope and ownership approved.

### Stage 2 — Data preparation

- Export source records.
- Validate mandatory fields.
- Identify duplicates.
- Reconcile active subscribers.
- Identify outstanding obligations.
- Build source-to-target mapping.
- Classify excluded records.

**Exit gate:** Data quality accepted.

### Stage 3 — Configuration

- Create or validate Collection.
- Configure Plans and pricing.
- Configure billing intervals.
- Confirm duration and end conditions.
- Confirm overdue and grace behavior.
- Validate Pricing Table or onboarding flow where applicable.
- Validate Member Center requirements.
- Confirm Billing Portal requirements.

**Exit gate:** Target configuration approved.

### Stage 4 — Integration and payment readiness

- Validate APIs.
- Test webhook processing.
- Confirm payment-method eligibility.
- Complete credential portability or reauthorization.
- Test billing creation.
- Test payment collection.
- Test failure and recovery behavior.

**Exit gate:** Technical and payment readiness approved.

### Stage 5 — Migration rehearsal

- Run a sample batch.
- Compare source and target records.
- Validate subscription states.
- Compare next billing dates.
- Confirm amounts.
- Validate payment authorization.
- Reconcile test transactions.

**Exit gate:** Rehearsal signed off.

### Stage 6 — Cutover

- Freeze or control source-system changes.
- Capture final migration snapshot.
- Process approved migration batch.
- Validate target records.
- Disable source billing for migrated records according to the cutover plan.
- Confirm no overlapping billing.
- Activate target billing only when all readiness conditions are met.

**Exit gate:** Merchant and DOKU approve production activation.

### Stage 7 — Hypercare

- Monitor billing events.
- Monitor payment outcomes.
- Monitor duplicate collection.
- Reconcile source and target populations.
- Handle customer reauthorization.
- Resolve exceptions.
- Report daily migration performance.

**Exit gate:** Stable operation and formal handover.

## 11. Migration controls and reconciliation

### Critical reconciliation dimensions

| Dimension | Comparison |
|---|---|
| Subscriber count | Source vs target |
| Active subscription count | Source vs target |
| Subscription status | Source vs target |
| Billing amount | Source vs target |
| Billing interval | Source vs target |
| Next billing date | Source vs target |
| Payment readiness | Eligible vs incomplete |
| Outstanding obligations | Source vs target treatment |
| Payment history | Historical record consistency |
| First post-migration collection | Expected vs actual |

### Recommended migration dashboard

| Metric | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Total in-scope subscribers | Migration population |
| Records extracted | Source completeness |
| Records validated | Data quality |
| Records migrated | Execution progress |
| Records rejected | Exception tracking |
| Payment-ready subscribers | Collection readiness |
| Reauthorization pending | Customer action tracking |
| First billing successful | Continuity measurement |
| Duplicate billing incidents | Critical risk |
| Unmatched transactions | Financial control |

### Essential controls

- Every migrated record must have a source identifier.
- Every target record must have a target identifier.
- Every migration batch must be traceable.
- Source billing must not overlap with target billing.
- Pending payments must not trigger unintended duplicate collection.
- Historical transactions must not be mistaken for new billing obligations.
- Failed records must enter an exception process.

## 12. Migration failure scenarios

| Scenario | Risk | Recommended action |
|---|---|---|
| Missing customer ID | Incorrect mapping | Quarantine record |
| Duplicate subscription | Double collection | Deduplicate before activation |
| Incorrect billing amount | Customer over/undercharge | Compare against source |
| Wrong next billing date | Early or late collection | Reconcile schedule |
| Token not portable | Auto-debit unavailable | Reauthorization |
| Member creation failure | Subscriber cannot be mapped | Retry or exception queue |
| Plan mismatch | Incorrect billing configuration | Correct target configuration |
| Webhook failure | Inconsistent payment status | Retry and reconciliation |
| Source billing remains active | Double collection | Controlled source shutdown |
| Target billing activated too early | Unauthorized collection | Activation gate |
| Customer cancellation during migration | Unwanted future charge | Final status recheck |
| Pending source payment | Duplicate collection | Resolve payment state first |

## 13. Merchant and customer communication

### Merchant communication

The merchant should receive:

- Migration scope and timeline.
- Data requirements.
- Payment authorization requirements.
- Test and rehearsal results.
- Cutover plan.
- Exception report.
- Reconciliation report.
- Hypercare and support process.

### Customer communication

Where customer action is required, explain:

- Why the billing experience is changing.
- Whether any action is required.
- Whether payment details need to be reauthorized.
- When the next payment is expected.
- Which merchant relationship is affected.
- Where to get support.

Customer communications must be approved by the merchant and comply with applicable consent and data-protection requirements.

